## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, the global energy crisis and environmental challenges caused by the consumption of fossil fuels are increasingly fierce, and the general demand for clean and sustainable energy is growing rapidly and continuously [1]. In the next generation of energy storage/conversion devices, the emergence of highly effective and renewable energy devices (supercapacitors [2], solar cells [3], secondary batteries [4], fuel cells [5], etc.) has attracted widespread concerns recently. However, the overall device efficiency of fuel cells and metal-air batteries is constrained by the sluggish kinetic process of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [6]. Because of the kinetic loss towards ORR of cathode, the voltage of metal batteries decreases logarithmically with increasing the loading current, which in turn leads to a low efficiency of electrochemical performance [7]. In principle, the ideal ORR catalysts should have the capability to efficiently break the $\scriptstyle0=0$ bonds, where Ptbased catalysts have been extensively recognized nowadays as excellent candidate materials [8].
Unfortunately, the drawbacks of noble-metal catalysts such as high cost, scarcity, poor durability, and low resistance of methanol have severely limited their large-scale applications [9]. Therefore, it is urgent to develop non-precious metal catalysts with high activity and stability [10], including transition-metal-based oxides, nitrides, phosphides, oxynitrides, carbides, chalcogenides, and metal-free heteroatom-doped carbon materials. The general structural characteristics of these electrocatalysts is polymer-to-carbon [11–13] or the combination of transition nitrogen-coordinated metals (Co, Fe, Mn, Ni, etc) and doped heteroatoms (N, S, P, B, etc) in the carbon matrix, which forms tremendously catalytic sites [14–20]. Additionally, the uneven charge distribution between metals and heteroatoms boosts the adsorption and reduction of oxygen, which is beneficial for catalyzing the ORR process. Also, the difference in electron spin density and electronegativity between carbon and heteroatoms has the same promotion effect towards ORR [21]. It is noteworthy that the agglomeration of large-particle metal-based catalysts during the calcination process results in a decrease of the stability. Previous studies reveal that pyrolyzing metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) composed of required transition metal ions and organic species is an effective strategy for fabricating the type of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{N}-C$ catalysts, where the formed active particles are usually small and highly dispersed [22–24]. Unfortunately, the limited carbon source of organic ligands and metal oxides involved in MOFs endows the catalysts with poor conductivity, meanwhile the robust structure may collapse/aggregate during the heat treatment, which causes the decrease of catalytic activities and stabilities [25]. Therefore, the application of MOF single-component derivatives for electrocatalysis is relatively hindered due to the intrinsic weak material properties. Undoubtedly, the establishing of rational strategies is of great importance to fabricate heterogeneous ORR catalysts with multinanocomponents, which can effectively promote the conductivity, catalytic activity, and long-term stability of single component derivatives of MOF. Hence, the marriage of MOF derivatives and carbonaceous matrices (carbon fibers [26], carbon nanotubes [27], polymer carbide [28], graphene [29], etc.) with high conductivities have certainly shown great advantages and promises. Actually, carbon materials have a significant importance in the field of catalysis because they are not only important reactants but also act as potential supports for catalyst particles, although the understanding of the pyrolysis for carbon-based precursors is relatively limited [30,31]. As we all know, hollow carbon spheres (HCS) usually display a high surface area, short mass/charge transfer path, and excellent conductivity. Therefore, it can be envisaged that MOF nanocrystals decorating on the surface of HCS will effectively alleviate the aggregation of MANAC active sites during the calcination and also excessively boost the conductivity of catalysts. This is the essential motivation underlying the present research. To the best of our knowledge, the electrochemical properties of MOF or MOFs derivatives decorated HCS structures have not been documented to date.
Taking into account the merits of HCS and MOF derivatives, the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalysts are fabricated in this work through a hydrothermal method, followed by an in situ confinement pyrolysis treatment. As illustrated in Fig. 1, the HCS matrix is firstly obtained through a condensation-polymerization reaction in the presence of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanospheres as template. Thereafter, the ZIF-67 nanocrystals with truncated-cube shape are grown on the surface of HCS by a hydrothermal method to generate the product HCS@ZIF-67. Subsequently, the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ products are isolated by an in situ pyrolysis of HCS@ZIF-67 precursors under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. In the structure, the HCS component not only exhibits a prominent conductivity but also serves as the substrate to effectively alleviate the agglomeration of active particles during the pyrolysis. As a result, the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst possesses a superior catalytic activity and long-term durability towards ORR in alkaline media to the benchmark $\sf P t/C$ .
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
All the chemicals were analytical grade and used without further purification. Typically, tetraethoxysilane (TEOS, $98\%$ , Aladdin), cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99\%$ , Sigma), 2- methylimidazole (2MIM, $98\%$ , Aladdin), methanol $\mathrm{\Phi}^{\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}}$ , $99.5\%$ , Aladdin), ammonium hydroxide $\mathrm{\langleNH_{4}.O H}$ , $25\%{\sim}28\%$ , Aladdin), resorcinol $(C_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , $99\%$ , Aladdin), formaldehyde (HCHO, Aladdin), hydrofluoric acid (HF, ${>}40\%$ , Aladdin), (3-aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES, $98\%$ Aladdin), hexadecyl-trimethylammonium bromide (CTAB, $99\%$ , Aladdin).
## 2.2. Synthesis of HCS
Hollow carbon spheres (HCS) were prepared through a condensation-polymerization reaction with the assistant of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanospheres as template [32]. Typically, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanospheres were synthesized by adding $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of tetraethoxysilane (TEOS) into an ethanol solution containing ammonium hydroxide. Resorcinol $_{(0.64\ \mathrm{g})}$ and formaldehyde $\mathrm{~\cdot~}1\mathrm{~mL}$ were then added to a mixed solution of deionized water $\mathrm{48~mL}$ , ethanol $(96~\mathrm{mL}$ , and ammonium hydroxide $\mathrm{2~mL}$ . Subsequently, the solution was sonicated for another $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanospheres were gently added to above solution and stirred for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The solution was transferred to a Teflonlined autoclave and kept at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the obtained polymer $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanospheres $(\mathsf{S i O}_{2}@\mathsf{R F})$ were calcined at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}\ {\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ . Finally, the HCS product was isolated after removing $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ templates from the heat-treated polymer $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ in $10\%$ HF acid for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 2.3. Surface modification of HCS
$_{0.02\mathrm{~g~}}$ of dried HCS was added in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of beaker filled with APTES/ethanol solution $\mathrm{(APTES}=2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , (3-aminopropyl)triethoxy silane $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ APTES, $98\%$ ), followed by sonicating for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then the solution was transferred to a Teflon-lined autoclave and kept at $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for overnight. The modified HCS products were washed with ethanol to eliminate excess APTES, and further dried in a vacuum oven at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ before use.
## 2.4. Synthesis of HCS@ZIF-67
$_{0.02\mathrm{~g~}}$ of surface-modified HCS was dissolved in a beaker containing $400~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of deionized water, and sonicated for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, $25.54\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-methylimidazole and $0.28\ \mathrm{g}$ of CTAB were dissolved in above solution to form solution A. $1.63\mathrm{~g~}$ of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate was added in $56~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and designated as solution B. Finally, the solution B was quickly poured into solution A and further stirred for $30s$ . The mixture solution was transferred to a Teflon-lined autoclave, and kept at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resultant product was centrifuged at 11000 rpm for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ , washed by methanol, and dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$
