## 2.7. Electrochemical measurements
The electrocatalytic performances were conducted on a ChenHua electrochemical workstation (CHI 760E) with a standard three-electrode system. The rotating disk electrode (RDE) with a diameter of $5\mathrm{mm}$ was used as the working electrode, while platinum wire and $\sf{A g/A g C l}$ were employed as the counter and reference electrode, respectively. The catalyst ink was prepared by ultrasonically dispersing $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalyst in a suspension consisting of $750\mathrm{uL}$ of absolute ethanol, $250{\mathrm{uL}}$ of water, and $25{\mathrm{~uL}}$ of naphthol. The catalyst film coated electrode was obtained by dispersing the catalyst ink on a glassy carbon rotating ring-disk electrode followed by drying in air. The catalyst loading on RDE was $250\upmu\mathrm{gcm}^{-2}$ for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ and $100\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $\sf P t/C$ All electrochemical tests were performed in $0.1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}$ solution at room temperature. 0.1 M KOH solution was bubbled with $0_{2}$ or $\Nu_{2}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ and kept in the same atmosphere during the measurement. All potentials in this work were measured against the $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ reference electrode and converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) reference scale by $\mathrm{E_{(RHE)}=E_{(A g/A g C l)}+0.059p H+0.205}$ A potential at which the current density exceeded a threshold value of $0.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was the onset potential $({\mathrm{E}}_{\mathrm{onset}})$ , while the potential corresponding to $50\%$ of the diffusion-limited current density was defined as the half-wave potential $\left(\mathsf E_{1/2}\right)$ . The electron transfer numbers $(n)$ of ORR at various potentials on the catalyst were accounted by using the following Koutecky-Levich formula.
$$
\frac{1}{J}=\frac{1}{J_{k}}+\frac{1}{0.62n F C_{0}{D_{0}}^{2/3}\upsilon^{1/6}\omega^{1/2}}
$$
Where $J$ is the measured current density, $J_{\mathrm{K}}$ is the kinetic limiting current density, $F$ is the Faraday constant $(96500\mathsf{C\ m o l^{-1}}.$ ), $C_{0}$ is the bulk concentration of oxygen, $D_{0}$ is the diffusion coefficient of oxygen in the bulk solution, $\upsilon$ is the kinematic Viscosity $(\nu\:=\:0.01\:\mathrm{\cm}^{2}\:\mathrm{\s}^{-1})$ , and $\omega$ is the angular velocity of the disk ( $\wp=2\pi N,$ N is the linear rotational speed). In 0.1 M KOH solution: $C_{0}=1.2\times10^{-3}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , $D_{0}=1.9\times10^{-5}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ [33].
The RRDE measurements were performed at a rotating speed of $1600\mathrm{rpm}$ at the sweep rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . Based on the ring current and disk currents, the electron transfer number $(n)$ and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ yield were determined by the following equations:
$$
n=4\frac{I_{d}}{I_{d}+\frac{I_{r}}{N}}
$$
$$
H_{2}O_{2}(\%)=200\frac{\frac{I_{r}}{N}}{I_{d}+\frac{I_{r}}{N}}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{d}}$ is disk current, $I_{\mathrm{r}}$ is ring current, and $N=0.37$ is the ring collection efficiency.
## 3. Results and discussion
The XRD patterns of as-prepared products are shown in Fig. 2a. The diffraction patterns of ${\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}@\mathbb{R}\mathbb{F}$ (Fig. S1) and ZIF-67 (Fig. 2a) signify the successful preparation of the corresponding material [34]. The broad peak around $23^{\circ}$ for the HCS sample is the characteristic diffraction pattern of carbon, which suggests that the precursor ${\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}@\mathsf{R F}$ has been completely carbonized after the pyrolyzing and etching treatment. For the HCS@ZIF-67 material, the diffraction patterns are composed of the characteristic peaks of ZIF-67 and HCS, demonstrating the collaboration of both components. The peaks located at $7.4^{\circ},10.5^{\circ}$ , and $12.8^{\circ}$ are assigned to the (110), (002), and (112) planes of ZIF-67, respectively. In the XRD pattern of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ , the peaks around $44^{\circ}$ and $51^{\circ}$ correspond to (111) and (20 0) planes of face-centered cubic cobalt, respectively (JCPDS 15-0806), confirming that the HCS@ZIF-67 precursor has been converted into ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ after the pyrolysis treatment. Besides, the characteristic peak of graphite carbon can also be observed in the patterns of the hybrid. To further analyze the content of metallic Co in the sample ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ , thermal-gravimetric (TG) analysis of the hybrid is conducted under air atmosphere (Fig. 2b). Owing to the oxidation of carbon, a pronounced weight loss in the temperature range of $300\sim600^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ is reasonably attributed to the combustion of nitrogen-doped carbon and the oxidation of Co to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The curve remains stable with further elevating the temperature and the final weight retention of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is approximately $25\%$ . Therefore, the content of metallic Co in the hybrid of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is calculated to be about $18.4\%$ .
SEM and TEM images of the products are depicted in Fig. S2 and Fig. 3. The surface of ${\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}@\mathsf{R F}$ nanospheres is smooth with an average diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S2a). No apparent morphology change is observed for the HCS sample except for a slightly rough surface (Fig. S2b), where the porous hollow structure can be clearly found with the thickness of carbon shell of about $50\mathrm{nm}$ from TEM image (Fig. 3a). For the HCS@ZIF-67 sample (Fig. S2e), the robust and continuous ZIF-67 nanocrystals are decorated on the surface of HCS. On the contrary, these isolated ZIF-67 NPs are disseminated on the surface HCS without surface modification in an island nucleation and anisotropic growth pattern (Fig. S3). The representative TEM image of HCS@ZIF-67 (Fig. 3b) further verifies that ZIF-67 NPs are tightly anchored to the surface of HCS. After a close observation, these ZIF-67 NPs on HCS are truncated cube and exhibit a much smaller particle size (Fig. 3c) than that of bare ZIF-67 (Fig. S2c) prepared in the absence of HCS. The significantly decreased particle size of ZIF-67 is reasonably ascribed to the fact that the HCS matrix effectively confines the growth of ZIF-67 crystals. SEM and TEM images of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ are shown in Fig. S2f and Fig. 3d, respectively. Actually, the carbonization process does not affect the spherical morphology. The crumpled shell and hollow interior of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is obviously discerned, and a highly porous texture is conspicuous throughout the shell network. A close observation of the shell for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ reveals that ultra-small NPs are encapsulated inside the HCS shell (Fig. 3e). These dispersed and ultra-small Co/NC NPs can offer a larger specific surface area and more accessible active sites, which greatly enhances the catalytic activity [25,35,36]. In sharp contrast, the ${C o/N C}$ particles obtained from the carbonization of bare ZIF-67 exhibit a severe aggregation together with a decreased particles size (Fig. S2d), significantly limiting the exposure of active sites. Therefore, the HCS substrate can effectively solve the aggregation problem of ZIF-67 NPs even after undergoing a calcination treatment. Furthermore, a distinct change is the existence of small pores on the surface of $\mathsf{H C S@C o/N C}$ , which may be related to the release of gas generated by the collapse of ZIF-67 skeleton [25,37]. The mass transfer of $0_{2}$ molecules during the ORR process is anticipated to be improved by the porous nature of carbon shells [38].
The structure and morphology of all catalysts are further analyzed by high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) images and element mapping distribution. More details of the nanostructure around the shell is depicted in Fig. 3f, which clearly indicates the existence of numerous Co NPs surrounded by the carbon matrix. Meanwhile, the Co/NC NPs are encapsulated on the shell of HCS with an average particle size of $\sim7~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . In fact, nitrogen-doped carbon derived from the carbonization of 2-methylimidazole ligands might restrict the growth of Co nanocrystallites [39]. Moreover, a few ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ particles with large size on HCS mainly might come from excess cobalt ions and organic ligands. Elemental mapping distribution of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ reveals an obvious dispersion of C, N, and Co species in the shell area with the agglomeration for partial Co particles (Fig. 3g). The HR-TEM image (Fig. 3h) of a single particle confirms that the well-defined lattice fringe of $0.207~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is assigned to the (11 1) planes of metallic cobalt, which is in well agreement with above XRD patterns.
The specific surface areas and pore size distributions of HCS and ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ are studied by nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms (Fig. 4a). Both isotherms are characteristic of type IV with a distinct hysteresis loop, indicating the existence of mesopores in adsorbents [40]. The pore size distributions obtained from the isotherms further indicate the mesoporous feature (Fig. 4b). The BET specific surface area of $\mathsf{H C S@C o/N C}$ is calculated to be $534~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , much smaller than $1045~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of HCS. This can be attributed to the mesoporosity deterioration during the secondary annealing of carbon sphere, together with the loading of ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ species. The porous texture of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is favorable for the mass transport of reactants and products during the ORR process [38].
Raman spectra are employed to explore the structure and assess the strain of HCS, HCS@ZIF-67, and ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ (Fig. 5a). The disorder-induced D band $1350\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ stretching G band $1590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) are observed for all samples [41]. For the HCS@ZIF-67 sample, the peaks in the range of $190\sim700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are reasonably associated to ZIF-67, including vibrational mode of $C o\mathrm{-N}$ and 2-methylimidazolate ligand [42,43]. Principally, the intensity ratio between G and D peak $\mathrm{(I_{G}/I_{D})}$ is an efficient method way to provide qualitative information about the crystallization degree of graphitic carbon, and acquire the knowledge of the edge plane exposure [44]. In generally, the graphitization contributes to electron conductivity. At the same time, the defects/dislocations are conducive to the doping of nitrogen, and further enhance the potential active sites for the adsorption of ORR intermediates and promote the catalytic activity [45]. The calculated $\mathrm{I_{G}/I_{D}}$ (integral intensity ratio) values of $\mathsf{H C S@C o/N C}$ , HCS@ZIF-67, and HCS are 0.536, 0.705, and 0.499, respectively, indicating that graphitic structure of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is developed compared to pure HCS. The higher graphitization is in favour of electron conductivity during the ORR process. Notably, a broad 2D band with low intensity around $2691~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the sample of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is also found, suggesting a multilayer graphene-like structure [46]. A thick graphitic structure not only provides more edge sites and defects which are preferential geometric structures for nitrogen doping, but also is more durable and more resistant to corrosion than a thin one [47]. Therefore, it’s demonstrated from Raman spectra that ${\mathrm{HCS@Co}}/$ NC offers a broad and low intensity of 2D band, therefore displaying a good electrocatalytic performance. The peak around $2929~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ next to 2D band is assigned to an extra band $\left(\mathsf{D}+\mathsf{G}\right)$ , and a shoulder between G and D band is characteristics of fewlayered graphene [46].
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ reveals the coexistence of Co, N, and C elements (Fig. S4). It can be observed from the high-resolution XPS spectrum of C 1s (Fig. 5b) the presence of CAC (283.3 eV) and CAN (284.2 eV) bonds, confirming that the nitrogen species have been encapsulated into the carbon network [48]. For the XPS spectrum of N 1s (Fig. 5c), there are three types of N species including pyridinic $(396.6~\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic $(397.4~\mathrm{eV})$ , and graphitic $(399.7{\mathrm{~eV}})$ nitrogen. Previous studies demonstrate that different nitrogen species provide diverse electronic environments for neighboring carbon, and also have a crucial influence on catalytic activities, where the presence of pyridinic N is fundamental for boosting the ORR activities [49]. The Co $2\mathsf{p}$ spectrum can be divided into several species (Fig. 5d). The peak centered at 777.1 and 792.7 eV are assigned to metallic Co, whereas the peaks at 778.8, 781.3, and $785.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ represent $C0^{3+}$ , $C o^{2+}$ , and satellite ones, respectively [50].
The electrocatalytic performances of all catalysts are characterized and analyzed in detail. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements are firstly examined in a 0.1 M KOH aqueous solution saturated with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 6a, the CV curves observed over a potential range from 0.2 to $1.2\mathrm{V}$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH aqueous solution is a quasi-rectangular one without redox peaks. In contrast, a sharp cathodic ORR peak is found in an $0_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ , indicating the presence of ORR activity [51]. Therefore, the electrocatalytic activity and kinetics of catalysts are explored by using Rotating disk electrode measurements. The evaluation of catalytic performance is based on the commercial $\sf P t/C$ as the benchmark for ORR. On the basis of half-wave potential, initial potential, and limiting current density, the catalytic ORR activity can be evaluated from LSV curves measured in $0_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . Pure HCS and ${C o/N C}$ catalysts exhibit poor catalytic activities with $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ values of $0.88{\mathrm{V}}$ and $0.98{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , and halfwave potential $\left(\operatorname{E}_{1/2}\right)$ of $0.76{\mathrm{V}}$ and $0.83\mathrm{V}$ , respectively (Fig. 6b). In sharp contrast, the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst has a better ORR activity, even higher than the state-of-the-art commercial $\sf P t/C$ in terms of half-wave potential, onset potential, and limiting current density $_{\textrm{\tiny{0.84\ V}}}$ vs. 0.82 V, 1.14 V vs. $_{0.96\mathrm{~V~}}$ , and $5.66\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ vs. $5.36\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively).
The remakable electrocatalytic performance of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ can be ascribed to the advantages of unique structure and multicomposition described as follows: 1) the catalysts employing HCS as the substrate can greatly improve the electron conductivity, straight-forwardly inhibit catalytically active NPs aggregation, and also maximize the efficiency of mass transfer; 2) a high density of active sites such as Co NPs and pyridine N are involved in the structure; 3) a large specific surface area of $534~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with optimal porosity facilitates the exposure of more active sites and promotes the diffusion of oxygen to active sites; 4) the structure of graphite shell encapsulating Co NPs perfectly protects active species from corrosion and maintains a high stability, meanwhile Co NPs can activate the surrounding graphite shell to make it have ORR activity [52].
It is observable that the onset potentials of LSV curves for the catalysts always remain fixed from rotating disk electrode measurements at different rotation rates in $0_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH solution (Fig. S5, Fig. S6a, Fig. S6c, Fig. S6e). The increased current densities with increasing the rotation may be caused by the enhancement of mass transfer at high rotation speeds, indicating a diffusion-controlled ORR process in alkaline electrolyte [51]. The linear increase in current density with rotating speed manifests a first-order kinetic towards ORR. The corresponding Koutecky-Levich (K-L) plots (Fig. 6c) of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ reveal the relationship between the inverse of current density $(j^{-1})$ and the inverse of square root of rotating speed $(\omega^{-1/2})$ at different potentials from 0.3 to $0.7~\mathrm{V}.$ . The electron-transfer numbers $(n)$ in ORR is determined by the K-L equation. The first-order reaction kinetics is relative to the concentration of dissolved oxygen and the almost constant n value of each oxygen molecule at different potentials, which results in the accomplishment of efficient reaction kinetics ORR kinetics. The n value for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is estimated to be $3.8\sim4.0$ at varied potentials, while the n values for ${C o/N C}$ and HCS are only about 2.5 and $3.0~\sim~3.5$ , respectively (Fig. S6b, Fig. S6d), implying their incomplete ORR processes. What’s more, the n value of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is close to the practical one of commercial Pt/C $3.7\sim3.9$ , Fig. S6f), indicating that the four-electron transfer path has a high selectivity, and the hydroxide ions $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ are stem from the reduction of dissolved oxygen molecules in the alkaline medium. The $n$ value of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is calculated to be $\sim3.8$ from the rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE) measurements (Fig. S7), which is in agreement with the RDE result (Fig. 6c) and confirms again the four-electrode ORR process. In addition, the ${\mathsf{H O}}_{2}^{-}$ yield is below $11.5\%$ in the range of $0.2\sim0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ , suggesting the four-electron pathway towards ORR. In order to further explore the excellent ORR activity of as-obtained catalysts, the measurements of LSV plots at low overpotentials are needed, where the ORR rate is determined by the surface reaction rate on electrocatalysts. The electronic conversion rate is reflected from Tafel slopes, which originates from the corresponding LSV plots at low overpotentials. As seen in Fig. 6d, the Tafel slope of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is $54~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ , which is slightly lower than those of Pt/C $(66\mathrm{~mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), HCS $(65\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}),$ , and $\mathsf{C o/N C}(68\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ . This result strongly proves that the faster electron transfer rate of the hybrid is the main reason for its excellent ORR efficiency. The electrochemically accessible active surface area (ECSA) of catalysts are subsequently evaluated, which is estimated from the electrochemical doublelayer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ [53]. The $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value is the slope of CV curves at various scan rates (Fig. S8). The calculated $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co}}/$ NC is $7.09~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , much larger than that of $\sf P t/C$ ( $1.93~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ), ${C o/N C}$ ( $6.13~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ ), and HCS $\left(3.84~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right.$ ), indicating that the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst has the largest ECSA value (Fig. 6e). The electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) are given in Fig. 6f. The electron transfer resistances $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ of $\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC,~Co/NC}$ and HCS are estimated to be 42.6, 41.7, $55.6\Omega$ , respectively. The $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ values of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ and ${C o/N C}$ are almost the same, whereas the slopes corresponding to the resistance of mass transfer in the low frequency region change significantly. This result emphasizes that the conductivity of hollow carbon sphere is not as good as that of pure ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ , which has been proved by above Raman spectra. Besides, the mass transfer resistance of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is lower than that of ${C o/N C}$ which is attributed to the deterioration of the mesoporosity confirmed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms.
The durability of catalysts should be also considered to investigate the practical application. The RDE curve of the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst possesses a half-wave potential shift of only $6~\mathrm{mV}$ and an almost constant onset potential after a potential cycling of working electrode for continuous 1000 potential cycles (Fig. 7a). In comparison, the RDE measurement of $\sf P t/C$ displays that Eonset and $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}$ have a more negative potential shift of $11~\mathrm{\mV}$ and $27~\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mV}}$ , respectively (Fig. 7b). Moreover, the decrease in diffusion-limiting current density for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is less noticeable than that of $\sf P t/C$ . The long-term stabilities of $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C},\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{N C}$ , and ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ are examined by the chronoamperometric responses at $-0.5{\mathrm{~V~}}$ in $0_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH solution for $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ (Fig. 7c). On one hand, about $90.6\%$ of original current density is retained for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ after the stability test. Such an abrupt drop of the current density in the very beginning has also been observed in other ORR electrocatalysts [54]. We speculate that the rapid drop in current over the first few milliseconds may be related to the reduction of pre-adsorbed oxygen species on the electrode surface before the application of the potential step [55], and the research along this line is ongoing in our lab. The current density of $\sf P t/C$ is reduced with retaining only $53.6\%$ of initial current density, further clarifying the excellent durability of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ . On the other hand, the ${C o/N C}$ catalyst shows a high current loss of $40.9\%$ after only $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . Consequently, it can be strongly concluded that ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is more durable and resistant to the corrosion than pristine ${C o/N C}$ because of the protective graphitic shells prohibiting Co NPs cores from leaching. SEM images of the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co}}/$ NC catalyst are further investigated after the durability test in the alkaline medium (Fig. 7d). Clearly, there was no distinguished alteration in the morphology even after the durability test, which is practically responsible for the outstanding cycling stability.
The catalysts of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ and $\sf P t/C$ are compared by measuring the methanol crossover via chronoamperometric responses for the practical implementation as cathode catalysts in methanolbased fuel cells (Fig. 7e). After injecting 2 M MeOH into $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution saturated with $0_{2}$ after $400s$ , there is an instant drop in current density for $\sf P t/C$ electrode because of MeOH oxidation on the surface of $\sf P t/C$ electrode. In sharp contrast, the current density has not changed dramatically for the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ electrode. Only $23\%$ of initial current density is preserved for $\sf P t/C$ compared to $82\%$ for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ after $1000s.$ . Therefore, the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst is more tolerance to methanol oxidation than $\sf P t/C$ . The tolerances of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ and $\sf P t/C$ to MeOH are emphasized by CV curves in the absence and presence of $2\mathrm{~M~}$ MeOH. The CV curve of $\sf P t/C$ displays a distinct variation in the current density with injecting methanol due to MeOH oxidation on the surface of electrode (Fig. S9a). Meanwhile, there is no variation in CV curves for ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ before and after the injection of MeOH (Fig. 7f). The CV curves of ${C o/N C}$ in the absence and presence of 2 M MeOH are responsible for the fact that ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ is not sensitive to MeOH (Fig. S9b). Above results indicate that the ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst has a strong resistance to MeOH. As show in Table S1, the electrocatalytic performances of ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ are comparable or superior to those of reported ORR catalysts.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, MOFs or MOFs derivatives anchored hollow carbon spheres (HCS) are applied in the field of electrochemistry for the first time. The matrix of HCS is firstly modified by ultrasmall ZIF67 nanocrystals which homogenously wrap into the shell through a hydrothermal method. Upon an in situ confinement pyrolysis treatment, the continuous ZIF-67 shells are completely converted into cobalt and nitrogen doped carbon, where those separated Co NPs are tightly surrounded by carbon layers. As a result, the resultant ${\mathrm{HCS@Co/NC}}$ catalyst demonstrates an intriguing ORR catalytic performance with high catalytic efficiency, selectivity for fourelectron transfer, tolerance toward methanol oxidation, and excellent long-term durability. The remarkable electrocatalytic performance towards ORR thanks to the advantages of unique structure and multi-composition, where abundant and highly dispersive active sites such as Co and N species are involved into the shells of HCS. It can be anticipated that the present methodology provides a great promise of developing extraordinary electrocatalysts as precious-metal alternatives, which derive from HCS and MOF derivatives by a low-cost approach for renewable energyrelated applications.
